00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.800
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel
and my name is Paul.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.720
Today's topic is the Afrikaans language.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.800
A couple of weeks ago I released a video on 
the Dutch language which you can find right here

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:27.340
This is kind of a follow-up to that video
because Afrikaans is a descendant of Dutch.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.460
That means that just like Dutch,
Afrikaans is a West-Germanic language.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:38.260
Afrikaans has a total of about 7.1 million native 
speakers, with 6.8 five million of those in South Africa.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.760
That's about 13.5 % of the South African population.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:48.000
That makes it the third most widely spoken language 
in South Africa and it's the majority language 
in the western half of the country.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.700
That means Northern Cape and Western Cape provinces.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:01:00.860
It's the native language of about 61% of white South Africans
and it's the native language of more than 3/4 of 
"colored" South Africans, about 3.4 million people.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.120
In South Africa, the term "colored" means
people of mixed racial background.
I didn't choose that term myself.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.860
It's also the native language of 4.6% 
of Asian South Africans.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.300
It's also the native language of 1.5% of black 
South Africans, that's about 600,000 people.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.840
And there are 10 to 15 million second language 
speakers in South Africa.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.960
In Namibia, it's the native language of 10 or 11% of 
people, including 60% of the white population.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.760
It's also widely used as a lingua franca and 
most people can speak it as a second or third language.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.080
So how did the story of Afrikaans begin?

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.160
Dutch-speaking settlers arrived 
in Southern Africa in the year 1652,

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:43.440
when the Dutch East India company set up a resupply 
station at Cape Town for its ships traveling to 
the indian subcontinent and to Southeast Asia.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.580
Their goal was to get fresh water, food 
and other supplies for their long sea journeys.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:54.760
When the local Khoisan people - people who speak 
Khoisan languages - were unwilling to meet 
the trade demands of the Dutch East India Company,

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.460
the company drove them out of the area into the inland 
and replace them with European settlers and with slaves 
from other parts of Africa.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:05.420
 

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.980
Most of the farmers, who were known as "Boers", were 
former laborers of the Dutch East India company,

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:13.500
and most of them were Dutch.
But some of them were French or German too.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:17.000
Upset with the conditions placed upon them 
by the Dutch East India Company,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.380
the European settlers began expanding inlands, so 
they could farm outside of the area controlled 
by the company.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:27.160
The Dutch East India Company wanted to prevent 
this inland expansion, so they outlawed the expansion,

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.780
ended European immigration 
and increase the number of slaves instead.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:35.720
Some of the slaves were brought from the Indian subcontinent 
and from present-day Malaysia and Indonesia.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.940
Britain seized control of the Cape Colony in 1815 
and began to import British settlers.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:46.180
The British eventually took steps to eliminate slavery 
and to put every restrictions on the Boer farmers.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:54.720
Large numbers of Boers migrated further inland 
to escape British rule setting up their own communities
and encountering various native African communities
 along the way.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:02.500
After a series of conflicts with the British called 
the "Boer Wars", South Africa became a unified 
British colony until its independence in 1961.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.520
But this is Langfocus. We're supposed to focus 
on languages. Why is all this history important?

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:13.340
Well, it is to help you imagine the conditions under
which the language spoken by those Dutch settlers 
changed and eventually became Afrikaans.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:20.680
Exactly how Afrikaans developed is a highly debated 
subject with some people insisting that
it's a natural direct descendant of Dutch,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.520
while other people insist that it's actually
a creole language that arose when slaves
and laborers attempted to learn Dutch.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.100
If you're not sure what creole languages are, you can have 
a look at my video on pidgin and creole languages right here.

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:39.620
Nobody knows exactly what process has caused 
Afrikaans to develop, but it's unlikely that either 
of those two theories is 100% true.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.720
In the early years of the Cape Colony, there was 
a continuous stream of Dutch-speaking immigrants,

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:46.700
as well as other Europeans who learned
Dutch as a second language.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:51.060
There were also many local speakers of Khoisan 
languages who learned Dutch as a second language.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.420
and there were also African and Asian slaves 
who learned Dutch as a second Language.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.940
Local Africans and slaves most likely spoke 
a Dutch-based pidgin language at some point.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.720
Typically a creole language arises 
when a pidgin language becomes 
the native language of the next generation.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:11.660
Usually without a lot of influence from speakers 
of the original standard language,
for example slave owners or colonial settlers.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:19.220
But, in the case of Afrikaans, there was always 
extensive contact with native speakers of Dutch.
More specifically, the emerging dialect called "Cape Dutch"

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:24.120
On top of that, Standard Dutch (and not Cape Dutch) 
was the language that was taught in schools.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.040
Both of these factors would help Cape Dutch remain 
relatively similar to standard Dutch.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.920
So even if Afrikaans is a creole language,
it's more like a partial creole or a semi creole language.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:40.920
2 creole languages that I'm familiar with are Tok Pisin 
and Bislama, which are spoken in Melanesia 
and they are English-based creoles.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:47.720
Both of those languages consists of
mainly English vocabulary but with the syntax, 
grammar and phonology of an Oceanic language.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.560
They're essentially new languages 
that got their vocabulary from English.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:59.440
Afrikaans is not like that. Its grammar became simplified 
but the syntax of the language is basically the same 
as Dutch and it's phonology is also close to Dutch.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:04.760
So it seems that Afrikaans developed from a form of Dutch
that was influenced by second language speakers,

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.360
rather than a completely new creole language 
featuring Dutch vocabulary.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.480
That's my personal assessment but I'm sure 
some people will disagree, and that's fine.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.720
Afrikaans continued to develop until it was legally 
recognized as its own language in 1925.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.000
So how is Afrikaans different from Dutch?

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:20.880
Loanwords

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.600
The vocabulary of Afrikaans was  influenced 
by the various languages that it came in contact with.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.320
For example, Malay. The word "amper " meaning "almost", 
comes from the Malay word "hampir"

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.380
The word "baie" meaning "much" 
comes from the Malay word "banyak".

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:41.180
Portuguese. The word "combers" meaning "blanket" 
comes from the portuguese word "cobertor"

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:45.960
The word "kraal" meaning "cattle enclosure" 
comes from the portuguese word "curral".

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.060
We have a similar word "corral" in English.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:53.160
Khoisan languages : 
the words for a lot of things in the local African 
environment came from Khoisan languages.

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.560
For example, plants &amp; animals.
This animal is called the khoedoe.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.880
But the overwhelming majority 
of Afrikaans vocabulary is Dutch.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.500
More than 90% or as high as 95%.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:03.140
Spelling

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:09.020
Afrikaans underwent some spelling simplifications 
to make the written language more reflective 
of the spoken language.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.680
I won't mention all the changes 
but here are some examples.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:16.560
The Dutch digraph "ij" pronounced "..."
became just a "y" in Afrikaans.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:21.380
So we have the Dutch word "prijs", 
which is spelt like this in Afrikaans "prys".

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:31.600
But Dutch words ending "lijk", pronounced as "..."
are spelt "lik". And that's a more accurate reflection 
of the real pronounciation.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:35.020
Dutch "tion" becomes "sion" in Afrikaans.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.420
So we have the Dutch word "nationaal"
and the speaking and the spelling in Afrikaans.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:47.320
These Dutch diphtongs which are actually pronounced 
identically by many Dutch speakers all became spelt "ou" 
in Afrikaans.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:54.040
So the Dutch words "vrouw" en "dauw"
are spelt like this in Afrikaans: "..."

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.280
There are also some other spelling changes
that represent new pronunciation in Afrikaans.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:04.120
For example, Afrikaans merge the Dutch consonnants 
"z" and "s" to a single sound spelt "s"

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:08.240
So the Dutch word "zorg" became "sorg" in Afrikaans.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:14.200
A similar phonetic change can also be found 
in the Northern Netherlands but it is not represented 
in writing. Only in speech.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:20.180
Afrikaans also merge together the Dutch sounds 
represented by "ch" and "g" to a single sound spelt "g".

00:07:20.280 --> 00:07:24.860
And again, a similar merging also be happened in the Netherlands but not in spelling, only in sound.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:33.080
In Afrikaans, when the g sound is preceded by an "s", 
it is written as "sk". So we have the Dutch word "school" 
and the Afrikaans word "skool".

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:37.680
Between 2 vowels, the Dutch "g" and "v" consonnants 
are omitted in Afrikaans.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:40.840
Look at these Dutch words "hoger", "regen"

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.600
And now the Afrikaans equivalents : "hoër", "reën"

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:50.920
Notice that the second vowel requires a diacritic so that we know to pronounce these as 2 separate vowels and not as 1 diphthong.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.280
There are many more spelling changes but 
those are a few examples to give you a sense 
of how things changed.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:55.920
Verbs

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.020
The verb system in Afrikaans 
is quite simple compared to Dutch.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:02.760
First of all, the simple past isn't used,
except for 8 basic verbs.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:07.400
For all other verbs, the perfect tense is used instead, which is also possible in Dutch too.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.660
So here is the Dutch sentence meaning "I watched".

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.660
And now here is the Afrikaans sentence.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:17.460
So notice that we used the perfect tense 
in Afrikaans, because there is no past tense.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:20.980
But this Afrikaans sentence is similar to 
the Dutch perfect tense sentence.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:25.840
But notice that the way the past participle 
is formed is a little different.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:28.700
Also notice that the form 
of the verb for "have" is different.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.580
These are both the present tense form 
of the verb meaning to have.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.340
The one in Dutch is conjugated for first person singular.
But Afrikaans verbs don't change according to personal number

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.840
So the Dutch verb will change in other sentences 
but, in Afrikaans, it will always be "het".

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:47.040
There is also no pluperfect tense in Afrikaans.
Pluperfect  is like "had watched".

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.120
Instead the perfect form would be used

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:54.060
So let's just jump into a few more example sentences
and look at these differences as well as some others.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.560
The next sentences mean "I talked to my children"

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:59.280
In Dutch: "..."

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.500
and in Afrikaans: "..."

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.340
First of all, notice the difference in personal pronouns. 
In Dutch "ik" and in Afrikaans: "ek"

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.180
and then notice the different forms 
of the verb meaning "to have"

00:09:11.180 --> 00:09:13.060
The next word "met" means "with"

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:19.000
and next, we can see that the possessive pronouns are
different. In Dutch "mijn" and in Afrikaans "my".

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.160
Next is the word for "children" but
notice that the plural form is different.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:28.060
Notice that the word order is exactly the same 
and that the second verb goes at the end in Afrikaans,

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:30.900
which is the same as in Dutch 
and also the same as in German.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:33.940
The next sentences mean:
"Did you have a good New Year?"

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:34.840
First, in Dutch: "..."

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.080
and in Afrikaans: "..."

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.080
In English, it's like :
"have you a good new year had?"

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.960
Again, we see the different forms 
of the verb meaning "to have".

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.740
Then we see the different spelling 
of the personal pronoun "jij" meaning "you".

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.920
Then, we have the indefinite article 
which has been shortened in Afrikaans.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.280
Then notice that the word for good is slightly different.

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:58.120
Then, after that, notice the slight difference 
in the words for "a new year".

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:02.560
And finally, the past participle of the verb meaning 
"to have" is the same in both sentences.

00:10:02.560 --> 00:10:05.260
Now let's look at a similar sentence 
but with a little change.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:12.880
This one means: "Did he have a good new year?". 
In Dutch: ...  and in Afrikaans: "..."

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.520
Look at the first word and notice
that the Dutch verb conjugation changes.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:20.940
This is the third person singular form of the verb 
but the verb form stays the same in Afrikaans.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:25.020
And next, we see that the personal pronoun "hij" 
meaning "he" is spelled differently.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:28.300
After that, the rest of the words are the same 
as in the sentence is up above.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:38.920
The next sentences mean "we will live, we will die" 
In Dutch: "..." and in Afrikaans : "..."

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.060
Notice here that the subject pronoun for "we" is different.

00:10:42.200 --> 00:10:47.720
In Dutch, the subject pronoun is "we"
but the object pronoun (similar to "us") is "ons".

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.140
In Afrikaans, "ons" is both the subject 
and the object pronoun.

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:55.580
Next, notice that the future tense in Dutch
is formed using "zullen" plus the infinitive.

00:10:55.660 --> 00:11:02.900
In Afrikaans, it's formed by using "sal" plus the verb.
And there really isn't an infinitive in Afrikaans,
there's just one single verb form.

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:05.440
This part here is the same as the present tense form.

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:11.920
So we can see that there are some differences 
between Dutch and Afrikaans but generally,
the vocabulary is very similar

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.700
and probably very easy to recognize once you're 
aware of those general differences.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.840
And the word order is basically the same.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.440
So how well can Dutch-speakers and
Afrikaans-speakers understand each other.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:27.000
The answer to this question depends on who 
you ask but generally they seem to be
mutually intelligible to a moderate level,

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:32.360
with Afrikaans speakers being able to understand Dutch 
better than Dutch speakers can understand Afrikaans.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:36.400
There are a couple of important factors in intelligibility. 
One of them is accent.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.820
I've heard from a few Dutch people and from a few 
Afrikaans people that they can understand 
certain speakers of the other language very well

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:45.140
but other speakers are harder to understand.

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:50.520
And that might be because certain Dutch dialects 
have pronunciation that is more similar 
to Afrikaans than others.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:56.740
And there are also a lot of second language speakers 
of Afrikaans whose accent might be difficult
for a native Dutch speakers to understand.

00:11:56.740 --> 00:12:00.480
Another factor is register. 
Register means the level of formality used.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:04.920
Now, if the speakers of both languages use 
the standard formal variety of their language

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:09.580
and they don't use any local variations or slang, 
then they'll probably understand each other much better.

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:15.740
Formal speech basically resembles the written language 
and the speakers of either language can basically 
understand the other one when it's written.

00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:20.700
A common question that I see written on the internet is 
"Should I learn Afrikaans before learning Dutch?"

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.940
"Would that help me to learn Dutch later on?"

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:28.400
I would say that that's probably a bad idea to learn 
Afrikaans just because you want to learn Dutch later.

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:31.680
It probably would end up confusing you
when you learn Dutch later on,

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.660
because Dutch is similar to Afrikaans 
but more complex.

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:39.860
I would say that the best reason to learn Afrikaans would be 
because you have a deep cultural interest in South Africa

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:43.040
and you want to spend some time in that country. 
Or in Namibia.

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.100
So the question of the day for speakers 
of Afrikaans and Dutch.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:50.640
How well can you guys understand each other?
Leave your comments and your experiences down below.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:51.940
And for everyone else:

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:56.840
I don't have a question of the day. So, why don't you ask 
a question to the Afrikaans and Dutch speakers?

00:12:56.840 --> 00:12:57.640
That sounds good!

